Eating Disorders (Anorexia/Bulimia)
Young Adult & Adolecence
Intense fear of weight gain, Distorted body image, Dietary restriction/Binge-purge cycle

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-013
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe, cramping muscle spasms, progressive lower limb weakness, and irregular heartbeat following an escalating period of severe purging.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acutely deteriorating patient exhibiting severe muscle cramping, inability to weight-bear, and subjective cardiac palpitations in the setting of known, escalating bulimic behaviours. Urgent after-hours clinical assessment was strictly necessary to assess neurological and cardiac status, identify likely critical hypokalaemia, and facilitate immediate emergency hospital admission to prevent fatal cardiac arrhythmias prior to the next regular GP session.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs are cramping so badly I can't walk, and my chest feels like it is skipping beats and fluttering constantly."
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a 24-hour history of severe, painful muscle spasms in the calves and thighs, progressing to an inability to safely bear weight. This is accompanied by frequent, disturbing cardiac palpitations. The patient admits to an intense fear of gaining weight and a severely distorted body image. Over the past two weeks, the patient has dramatically escalated inappropriate compensatory behaviours, engaging in severe dietary restriction during the day, followed by recurrent episodes of binge eating and self-induced vomiting up to six times every evening.
Functional Status: Currently unable to ambulate due to severe muscle cramping and weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of imminent cardiac event; parents are present but unable to safely transport the patient due to the severity of the leg cramps.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 52 (Resting), irregular | Temp: 36.2 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 46 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious, fatigued, and is clutching their lower extremities during waves of visible muscle fasciculations.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular examination reveals a slow, irregularly irregular pulse. Neurological examination demonstrates generalized proximal muscle weakness and diminished deep tendon reflexes bilaterally. Skin shows marked xerosis. The dorsal surface of the right hand exhibits pronounced calluses (Russell's sign). Oral cavity shows significant dental enamel erosion and bilateral parotid gland hypertrophy. The patient reports secondary amenorrhoea for the past four months.
Environmental Observations: The bathroom sink shows signs of recent, poorly cleared emesis; numerous empty food wrappers are hidden under the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil prescribed medications. Patient admits to daily senna use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Critical Hypokalaemia/Electrolyte Derangement secondary to Severe Eating Disorder (Bulimia Nervosa) - Status: Medical Emergency
Differentials:

Primary Neuromuscular Disease (e.g., Myopathy) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the acute onset of cramps coupled with palpitations, and the overwhelming context of severe, repetitive purging behaviours.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the bilaterally symmetric nature of the cramping, the presence of generalized weakness, and the systemic symptom of palpitations.

Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit admission of self-induced vomiting for weight control, the lack of fever, and the pathognomonic physical signs of chronic purging (Russell's sign, parotid hypertrophy).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (High risk of fatal arrhythmia)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe muscle cramping, generalized weakness, and an irregularly irregular bradycardia. In the setting of an escalating binge-purge cycle, these objective findings are highly indicative of severe hypokalaemia, hypochloraemia, and a metabolic alkalosis. The cardiac irregularity suggests an evolving, potentially fatal arrhythmia. Deferring care until standard daytime hours was categorically unsafe; the immediate after-hours physical assessment confirmed severe systemic compromise and mandated an immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for continuous cardiac monitoring, an urgent electrocardiogram to detect bradycardia or QTc prolongation, and intravenous electrolyte replacement.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate escalation to the Emergency Department via ambulance (000 called by clinician). Advised the receiving ED of the suspected critical hypokalaemia and need for urgent ECG and serum electrolyte panel.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent further cardiac strain, remain completely at rest on the bed while awaiting the ambulance, explicitly understanding that physical exertion with depleted electrolytes can trigger a fatal heart rhythm. To allow the medical team to stabilize your heart, disclose the exact frequency of vomiting and laxative use to the ED doctors, recognizing that accurate information dictates the precise amount of intravenous potassium required to save your life.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None. Immediate ED transfer.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent multi-disciplinary review involving GP, psychiatry, and dietetics immediately upon medical discharge from the hospital.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. Parents instructed to immediately commence CPR if the patient loses consciousness and stops breathing normally prior to paramedic arrival.

PATIENT INFORMATION

